# Median family net wealth: white vs black and Hispanic respondents, NLSY79, 1985-1996

**Hypothesis (data-grounded answer to the question).** In this NLSY79 cohort-sequential sample, the *family*
net wealth (respondent/spouse unit; the only wealth construct in the file) of white respondents - operationalised
as `Racial/ethnic cohort, 1979 == 3` (NON-BLACK NON-HISPANIC) - exceeded that of black (`1:BLACK`) and Hispanic
(`2:HISPANIC`) respondents in **every wave from 1985 onward, and the disparity widened sharply over the eleven
years**. Unweighted complete-case medians (nominal-year dollars) were:

| wave | Black (n) | Hispanic (n) | White, non-Hispanic (n) | white:black | white:Hispanic | $ gap W-B | $ gap W-H |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1985 | $1,600 (1,699) | $500 (2,631) | $3,500 (5,774) | 2.19x | 7.00x | $1,900 | $3,000 |
| 1990 | $4,800 (1,528) | $1,600 (2,431) | $12,972 (5,336) | 2.70x | 8.11x | $8,172 | $11,372 |
| 1996 | $9,800 (1,368) | $2,562.50 (2,114) | $42,600 (3,557) | 4.35x | 16.62x | $32,800 | $40,038 |

**The ratio and the absolute dollar gap move in the same direction: both widen.** white:black rose 2.19 -> 2.70 ->
4.35 (bootstrap 95% CI 1985 [1.84, 2.47] vs 1996 [3.68, 5.25]; non-overlapping) and white:Hispanic 7.00 -> 8.11 ->
16.62 ([5.80, 8.75] vs [13.73, 21.75]), while the dollar gaps grew from $1,900/$3,000 to $32,800/$40,038.

**Robustness.** (i) Balanced panel observed in all three waves (n=5,739): white:black 2.38 -> 3.60 -> 4.69 and
white:Hispanic 12.67 -> 12.29 -> 17.75, dollar gap W-B $2,200 -> $33,525 - so the widening is not an artefact of the
44.5% attrition in the 1996 wealth measure. (ii) p99 winsorising leaves medians and ratios numerically unchanged,
because the supplied wealth variables are already top-coded near the pooled p99 (1996 max = p99 = $1,232,183, 141
ties), so the widening is not a top-tail outlier artefact. (iii) Constant 1996 dollars (external CPI-U
1985=107.6, 1990=130.7, 1996=156.9): within-wave ratios are invariant to a common deflator, and the real gap still
widens ($2,771 -> $32,800 white:black). Real medians grew for all three cohorts - black x4.20, Hispanic x3.52,
white x8.35 (nominal: x6.13 / x5.13 / x12.17) - so the divergence reflects *relative rates of wealth accumulation*
over early adulthood, not absolute immiseration of any cohort.

**Where in the distribution.** The shortfall is distribution-wide but largest at and below the median: 1996
white:cohort ratios are 4.35 / 16.62 at the median, 2.52 / 6.55 at p75 and 2.10 / 4.39 at p90. This is mirrored by
the incidence of zero-or-negative net wealth in 1996: black 20.8%, Hispanic 34.9%, white 10.9%; and by p25, which
is $0 for Hispanic respondents in all three waves and for black respondents in 1985 and 1990 (rising to $1,000 in
1996), versus $500 / $1,665 / $8,500 for white respondents.

**Secondary, lower-confidence composition check** (blank read as "owned none of this asset" = 0, an assumption,
with ownership rates reported beside every mean). By 1996, zero-filled *observed* asset means for black respondents
had nearly converged on white levels - residential property $40,191 (36.8% owning) vs $44,902 (37.4%), vehicles
$7,010 vs $7,178, items over $500 $5,327 vs $6,026 - even though median net wealth was still 4.35x lower. Farm/
business/other property (only 4-6% reporting) remains descriptive. For Hispanic respondents a large observed gap
persists, led by residential property ($18,929, 23.5% owning vs $44,902, 37.4%). Because the file contains **no
financial assets (savings, stocks, IRA/401k) and no debt detail**, the reading is that the remaining black-white
net-wealth gap in 1996 is carried by liabilities and by unobserved financial assets, while the Hispanic gap
additionally reflects much lower homeownership - a hypothesis, not an identified finding.

**Scope limits.** Unweighted analysis; the file has no sampling weights and Sample IDs map 1:1 to race cohorts
(so they cannot identify military vs civilian subsamples, and no respondents were excluded on that basis). Pooled
all-respondent figures are therefore not population-representative; within-cohort medians and cross-cohort ratios
are the interpretable quantities. Blanks in the wealth variables (20.4% / 26.7% / 44.5% in 1985 / 1990 / 1996)
are treated as non-response and dropped, never zero-filled - exact zeros are far more common for black and Hispanic
respondents (1996: 10.1% and 22.4% vs 2.4%), so zero-filling would have fabricated mass at zero. Wealth is family/
spouse-unit net wealth, not individual wealth; respondents were 14-22 in 1979, so these are pre-peak-of-wealth
life stages. No causal interpretation: within-cohort change over an 11-year window that also includes the early-1990s
recession and a housing/credit cycle.
